Miriam,

I reran the richness models with site as a random intercept and restoration a=
ge as
a smoothed term (k =3D 5) rather than the linear fit we had in the March draf=
t. The
result is cleaner than I expected:

  * Rarefied species richness rises steeply for the first 6-8 years and is fl=
at
    after about year 10 (edf =3D 2.7, p < 0.001).
  * The ground-nesting specialists (Andrena, Calliopsis, the Melissodes we can
    resolve to species) do NOT plateau. They are still climbing at 25 years, =
and
    the two oldest restorations (SBP, KRR) are still below the Ritter remnant.
  * Pan-trap colour interacts with year, but it does not change the shape of =
the
    curve, so I think we can report blue/yellow/white pooled and put the colo=
ur
    model in the supplement.

The uncomfortable part: if we drop BRF (the remnant) the specialist result lo=
ses
most of its power, because the remnant anchors the top end. Reviewers will ask
about that. I'd rather we say it up front than have it pulled out of us.

Code is in the shared folder under analysis/gam_richness_v4.R. I fixed the
offset for unequal trapping effort (we were using bowl-days without removing
tipped bowls).

